ause of the apple and that  is notice this about this apple  this apple  if it falls to the ground  and becomes properly embedded within the  soil and receives enough rain  will start to grow  into another apple tree  it has therefore what aristotle calls  the potency  it has the potency in it to become  something more mature more developed it  is then  and this is aristotle's great innovation  what is an apple  aristotle will tell us an apple is an  immature  apple tree  this is an apple tree  at a very high degree of potency and a  low degree of actuality when this apple  grows into a full apple tree it is the  most what an apple is its potency has  been actualized  as aristotle understands it  those causes go back  eternally  the world eternally goes back  in efficient causes  but aristotle doesn't think  that  we could have that world without some  kind of ultimate  causal explanation  and aristotle doesn't think  it can be an efficient cause at the  beginning of time because he thinks  those causes can't go back eternally  so he explains it in terms of a final  cause  aristotle looks at all the changes or  motions in the cosmos and he notices an  order among them  beginning with the sublunar causes in  our world and then looking at the  motions of the heavens he sees them as  causing the things below them  he suggests that for all of that  infinite series of causation there must  be some kind of cause which is not an  efficient cause but which is a final  cause and it is itself not caused by  anything else otherwise you need an  explanation for that cause  he calls this cause the prime mover or  the unmoved mover and he spends  extensive time explaining and defending  and arguing for  the fact that we need to have a prime  mover to make sense of causality  how could the unmover cause the movement  of everything else  think about how  the apple and the tree might move you  the apple just sits in the tree  it doesn't push you  it draws you  you see the apple and 